#
# GRIND cycle 10 — two filings, both about a number a generated document
# published beside prose describing that number, and in both cases the prose
# and the number could not both be true.
#
# D-175 (two-derivations-of-one-number-disagree). foundry_state.derive_cycle_
# count published index + 1 over the server counter, and that counter advances
# at inspect_start, so it counts INSPECTs and not GRINDs. On a halted run the
# GRIND at index + 1 is precisely the one the cap refused to open. Driven end
# to end at the real doors with --max-cycles 2: Foundry-Phase('grind_start')
# returned ok, wrote phase HALTED and the reason "opening GRIND cycle 3 would
# exceed it" — so two GRIND cycles ran, which is _halt_if_capped's own stated
# arithmetic — and generated the report INSIDE that same call, whose
# baseline_comparison.current.grind_cycles read 3 and whose REPORT.md rendered
# "| GRIND cycles | 22 |  | 12 | 3 |" beside run.max_cycles 2 in the same
# document. One transition wrote both numbers and they contradicted each other
# about the one quantity the cap is defined over; foundry_report and
# scripts/measure-run.py both reach that number through the shared derivation,
# so both published the contradiction.
#
# The halt now subtracts exactly the GRIND it prevented and nothing else, keyed
# on halted_at_cycle / halted_reason — the two DATA fields _halt_if_capped
# writes beside the phase, because foundry_state may not import vocab and
# re-typing RUN_PHASE_HALTED there is the hand-copied-enum drift the house rule
# bans. Re-basing the whole derivation on phase_history's F3 entries was the
# alternative and it is worse: thunder-viper's counter never moved and its
# history carries no per-cycle F3 stamps, so the two archives that calibrate
# this function would stop reproducing their own published numbers. A run at
# the same counter with no halt record still publishes index + 1, which is what
# the second half of the transcript below shows.
#
# D-172 (unreported-accounting-derives-the-cycle-axis-from-a-proxy, and the
# report half of generated-prose-states-null-as-fact). The unreported-dispatch
# by_cycle axis is built from cycles_of_agent, which can only come from
# stream-rollup.json, whose buckets ARE keyed by the server counter. spawns.log
# records a teammate dispatch as timestamp, casting_id, phase, wave and
# prompt_hash with no cycle anywhere in the row, so a CAST or GRIND teammate
# can never be attributed to a cycle by any reader of that ledger. Driven at
# the door over the live archive: 19 unreported pairs, 14 of them teammates
# such as casting-1@F1 and casting-1@F3, and the axis named ZERO teammates in
# any of cycles 0-9 — while the generated sentence beside that column stated it
# "names the cycles those agents were dispatched in", false for 14 of the 19.
# Driven on the synthetic shape the filing also names, three teammates
# dispatched at cycle 1 with one reporting spend, REPORT.md rendered the cycle
# row as 0 unreported while the phase row correctly rendered 2.
#
# No stamp is invented here. Correlating a spawn timestamp against
# phase_history's cycle windows would be a second proxy, and "the cycle axis is
# derived from a proxy" is the filing. The axis keeps its one real source, the
# summary returns the pairs that source cannot cover as pairs_without_cycle,
# the section publishes their number as unreported_without_cycle, and the
# sentence states that scope instead of a coverage it does not have. The field
# that would widen the axis — a per-dispatch `cycle` on every spawns.log row —
# belongs to the spawn writer and was relayed to the lead rather than faked.
#
# Every archive read here is synthesized under pytest's tmp_path by the test
# itself, because foundry-archive/ is git-ignored and this log is re-executed
# inside a `git worktree add --detach` checkout that holds tracked files only.
# No tempdir path, clock reading or SHA reaches the output.


